  [1mB. Per-task [38;5;153msetTimeout[39m.[0m On create/edit, schedule timers for each boundary. Millisecond-precise, no polling. But timers
  die on reload (reschedule everything on load), browsers throttle them hard in background tabs, laptop sleep silently
  eats them, and [38;5;153msetTimeout[39m clamps around 24.8 days — a due date next month never fires. Worst of all, catch-up needs an
  entirely separate code path, so you maintain two implementations of one behavior and they will drift.

  [1mC. Pure derived state.[0m No scheduler. Each render computes what's due-soon and overdue from task data plus the current
  time. Elegant, nothing persisted. But it can't express "notify once" — a toast is an [3medge[0m, derived state is a [3mlevel[0m,
  so you'd get a permanent banner instead of a notification. And dismissal has to persist anyway, which smuggles the
  fire-log back in.

  [1mRecommendation: A, with C for one specific job.[0m The fire-log is the load-bearing idea — it's what makes "fire exactly
  once" survive reloads and what collapses catch-up into normal operation. But C is genuinely right for the [3moverdue [0m
  [3mbadge in the list[0m, which is a level ("this task is late") rather than an edge ("this task just became late"). So:
  derived state renders badges, the fire-log drives toasts. Two concepts because there are honestly two behaviors, not
  two mechanisms for one.

  B I'd discard outright — the background-tab throttling alone disqualifies it for a reminder system.

